<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-connected-top-spaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Connected topological spaces</title>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-def-separatedness">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>X</m> is a nonempty topological space. Following statements are equivalent.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    There exists nonempty open subsets <m>U,V</m> of <m>X</m> such that <m>U\cap V=\emptyset</m> and <m>X=U\cup V</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    There exists nonempty closed subsets <m>A,B</m> of <m>X</m> such that <m>A\cap B=\emptyset</m> and <m>X=A\cup B</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    There are nonempty subsets <m>C,D</m> of <m>X</m> such that <m>X=C\cup D</m>. Furthermore, limit points of <m>C</m> are not contained in <m>D</m> and limit points of <m>D</m> are not contained in <m>C</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <proof>
    <p>
        (1)<m>\implies</m> (2). The complement of <m>U</m> (resp., the complement of <m>V</m>) is an open subset <m>V</m> (resp., <m>U</m>). Therefore, <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are both open and closed subsets. Hence, we can take <m>A=U</m> and <m>B=V</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
       (2)<m>\implies</m> (1). The complement of <m>A</m> (resp., the complement of <m>B</m>) is a closed subset <m>B</m> (resp., <m>A</m>). Therefore, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are both open and closed subsets. Hence, we can take <m>U=A</m> and <m>V=B</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
</section>